---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnicodeCharacterLookup from '@/components/tools/reference/UnicodeCharacterLookup.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="unicode-character-lookup">
  <UnicodeCharacterLookup client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the Unicode character lookup</h2>
    <p>
      Paste text to see every character in it, one tile each. Grey tiles are characters that do not show
      on their own: spaces, zero-width characters, direction marks, controls and combining accents. Select
      a tile to see the character's code point, official name, general category, block and its bytes in
      UTF-8 and UTF-16, with the escapes for HTML, JavaScript and CSS.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also type code points instead of text: <code>U+00E9</code>, <code>0xE9</code>,
      <code>&amp;#233;</code> or <code>\u&#123;1F600&#125;</code>, several separated by spaces. Switch to
      search by name to find a character such as "right arrow" or "greek small letter alpha".
    </p>
    <h2>Find invisible characters</h2>
    <p>
      Copied text can carry characters you cannot see: a zero-width space (U+200B), a non-breaking space
      (U+00A0), a byte order mark (U+FEFF) or a right-to-left override (U+202E). They break searches,
      code and passwords. The lookup shows each one as its own tile.
    </p>
    <h2>Data</h2>
    <p>
      Names, categories and blocks come from the Unicode Character Database. The page loads the names
      in parts of 4,096 code points, so a lookup loads only the part it needs.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
